{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794644604,"nonce":"IPpj70ceFueJOAf5ssPUVLh5okY=","message":"Landed in 3ef74fc4. The `css_window_check` mode that measures it is a9e54f8a.\n\nA bound opacity on a node that paints one primitive is painted without a group, so a row carries the alpha. `RenderTree::fades_without_group` decides: a childless div with a solid background on the background layer and no border, outline, shadow, effect, blend, mask, filter, 3D or glass. A CSS keyframe animation of opacity, alone or with transform, on such a node is carried by a binding as the transform ones are. cn skeletons and the indeterminate progress bar qualify: `cn_skeletons_fade_on_rows` has 40 of 40 frames on rows and no pixel differing from the full paint.\n\n140 plain fading blocks.\n- Headless, a frame: rows 2.05 ms, baked 11.5 ms, full paint 14.3 ms. Growth over 60 frames: 0 MB on rows, 16 MB baked (56 MB resident before the run against 242 MB).\n- Window, CPU as the slope of `ps` cputime between 10 s and 30 s of a running process (the totals from `time -l` swing by more than the signal because of startup): at a 30 fps cap rows 0.05 cores and baked 0.35 to 0.39 over two runs each; at 60 fps 0.10 against 0.64; uncapped 0.14 against 0.92.\n- Peak footprint from `time -l`: 83 MB against about 1.0 GB.\n\nA fade from nothing stayed on rows for all 20 frames of its harness scene. A node painted while at exactly zero has no ratio to grow from, so a row declines it and the next frame is a full paint; I have not seen that in a window.\n\nWhat it does not cover is filed as its own issue: nodes that paint more than one draw.","files":null}]}